.\" .br - line break (nothing else on the line)
.\" .B  - bold
.\" .I  - green or kursive (on HTML)
.\" .TP - paragraph ? (header line, followed by indented lines)
.\"
.TH igmpproxy.conf 5 "" "@PACKAGE_STRING@"
.SH NAME
igmpproxy.conf \- Configuration file for
.BR igmpproxy (8)
multicast daemon

.SH DESCRIPTION
.B igmpproxy.conf
contains the configuration for the 
.B igmpproxy
multicast daemon. It defines which network interfaces should be
used by the routing daemon. Each interface must be give one of the following roles:
.B upstream
,
.BR downstream ,
.B querier
or
.B disabled
.

The
.B upstream
network interface is the outgoing interface which is responsible for communicating
to available multicast data sources. Several upstream interfaces are allowed (for
example the internet and a separate IPTV VLAN): a group is requested on every upstream
interface whose whitelist and blacklist allow it, and a stream gets a route from the
upstream interface it actually arrives on.

.B Downstream
network interfaces are the distribution interfaces to the destination networks, 
where multicast clients can join groups and receive multicast data. When there are
downstream interfaces, at least one upstream interface must be configured.

On a
.B querier
network interface igmpproxy only acts as the IGMP querier: it sends general queries,
and after a leave group specific queries, so the IGMP snooping switches on that network
deliver multicast only to the ports that asked for it. Nothing is forwarded to or from
a querier interface and nothing is requested upstream for it. A configuration with only
querier interfaces needs no upstream interface.

On
.B disabled
network interfaces all IGMP or multicast traffic is ignored altogether. If multiple
IP addresses is used on one single interface (ae. eth0:1 ...), all interface
aliases not in use should be configured as disabled.

Any line in the configuration file starting with
.B #
is treated as a comment. Keywords and parameters can be distributed over many lines.
The configuration file has four main keywords:

.B chroot
.I directory
.RS
Changes the apparent root directory to the given path after startup, thus
denying
.B igmpproxy
access to files and commands outside that environmental directory tree.
.RE


.B user
.I username
.RS
Specifies the userid to which
.B igmpproxy
will change after startup.
.B igmpproxy
must be started as root, but it will drop root privileges to the specified
user.
.RE


.B quickleave
.RS
Legacy option, same as
.BR "fastleave auto" .
.RE

.SH TIMER AND FAST LEAVE OPTIONS (igmpproxy-plus)
Times accept a unit:
.I 500ms ,
.I 2s
(or a bare number of seconds, fractions allowed) and
.I 2m .
The options in this section are global and must appear before the first
.BR phyint .
The ones marked (also phyint) can be repeated inside a
.B phyint
block to override the global value for that downstream interface.

.B fastleave
.IR off | on | auto
(also phyint)
.RS
What happens when a host leaves a group.
.B off
(default) follows RFC 2236: the proxy sends
.B lastmembercount
group specific queries
.B lastmemberinterval
apart and stops forwarding on that interface if nobody answers.
.B on
stops forwarding on that interface immediately; the queries are still sent so
other receivers on the same segment re-join within milliseconds.
.B auto
stops immediately when the leaving host was the last host the proxy saw
reporting on that interface, otherwise it behaves like
.BR off .
Host tracking needs hosts to see each other's reports suppressed, which is the
case on a switch with IGMP snooping.
.RE

.B lastmemberinterval
.I time
(also phyint)
.RS
Time between last member queries, 100ms to 25.5s. Default 1s (stock igmpproxy
used 10s).
.RE

.B lastmembercount
.I n
(also phyint)
.RS
Number of last member queries, 1 to 10. Default: robustness.
.RE

.B robustness
.I n
(also phyint)
.RS
1 to 7, default 2. Group membership timeout is
robustness * queryinterval + queryresponseinterval.
.RE

.B queryinterval
.I time
.RS
Interval between general queries, default 125s.
.RE

.B queryresponseinterval
.I time
.RS
Max response time advertised in general queries, 1s to 25.5s, default 10s.
.RE

.B startupqueryinterval
.I time
,
.B startupquerycount
.I n
.RS
General queries sent right after start. Defaults: queryinterval/4 and robustness.
.RE

.B grouptimeout
.I time
(phyint only)
.RS
Overrides the computed group membership timeout on that interface.
.RE

.B upstreamleavedelay
.I time
.RS
Keep the upstream membership this long after the last downstream member is
gone, 0 (default) to 5m. A join inside this window resumes forwarding at once
without a new upstream join.
.RE

.B queryversion
.IR 2 | 3
(also phyint)
.RS
IGMP version of the queries sent on downstream interfaces, default 3. With IGMPv3
queries hosts stay in IGMPv3 and never suppress each other's reports, so every host
is tracked and every leave arrives. IGMPv1/v2 hosts keep working: when one reports,
that group runs in IGMPv2 compatibility on that interface for the group membership
interval (RFC 3376 7.3.2), and
.B fastleave auto
then behaves like
.BR off .
.RE

.B hostlimit
.I n
.RS
Hosts tracked per group per interface, default 256. Beyond it auto fast leave
falls back to the RFC behaviour for that group and interface.
.RE

.B controlsocket
.IR path | none
.RS
UNIX socket that returns the live group table as JSON, default
/var/run/igmpproxy.sock. Read it with
.BR "igmpproxy -S" .
.B "igmpproxy -Q"
sends a general query through it, so every member reports again.
.RE

.B maxmemberships
.I count
.RS
Linux only: raise net.ipv4.igmp_max_memberships to at least this at start.
The proxy joins every forwarded group upstream on one socket, and the
kernel default of 20 would leave the 21st channel unrequested. Default
1024, 0 leaves the system setting alone.
.RE

.B eventlog
.IR path | off
.RS
Write an event log: one JSON object per line for every join, leave,
start and stop of forwarding (with the reason), host timeout, IGMPv2
compatibility change, upstream join and leave, and query. Each line has
"ts" (UTC), "ev", and where it applies "group", "if" and "host". Read it
with
.BR "igmpproxy -E" ,
which prints the rotated file followed by the current one. Off by default.
.RE

.B eventlogsize
.I KiB
.RS
Rotate the event log at this size: the file is renamed to
.IR path .1
(replacing the previous one), so at most twice this size is kept.
Default 1024, 0 never rotates.
.RE

.B eventlogreports
.BR on | off
.RS
Also log the periodic reports of members that are already known and the
periodic general queries. Default on.
.RE

.B querierelection
.BR on | off
.RS
Querier election (RFC 2236 section 3, RFC 3376 section 6.6.2) on downstream and querier
interfaces. Default off: igmpproxy always sends general queries. On: while another
router with a lower address sends queries on an interface, igmpproxy sends no general
queries there and uses the other querier's query interval and robustness for its
membership timers; when that router has been silent for the Other Querier Present
Interval (robustness x query interval + query response interval / 2) igmpproxy is the
querier again and queries at once. On downstream interfaces igmpproxy still sends its
own group specific queries after a leave, so how fast a stream stops does not depend on
another router. Can also be set per phyint.
.RE

.B stalltimeout
.I time
.RS
A forwarded stream that carried at least 500 kbit/s and then has no data for this long
while members receive it is reported as stalled (event
.BR stream_stalled ,
.B stalled_ms
in the status). Default 10s, 0 = off.
.RE

.B hashtablesize
.I size
.RS
Obsolete, accepted and ignored.
.RE


.B phyint 
.I interface
.I role 
[ ratelimit 
.I limit
] [ threshold 
.I ttl
] [ altnet 
.I networkaddr ... 
]
.RS
Defines the state and settings of a network interface.
.RE

.SH PHYINT OPTIONS

.B interface
.RS
The name of the interface the settings are for. This option is required for
phyint settings.
.RE

.B role
.RS
The role of the interface. This should be either
.B upstream
(only one interface),
.B downstream
(one or more interfaces),
.B querier
(query only, nothing forwarded) or
.B disabled
\&. This option is required.
.RE

.B ratelimit
.I limit
.RS
Defines a ratelimit for the network interface. If ratelimit is set to 0 (default),
no ratelimit will be applied. This setting is optional.
.RE

.B threshold
.I ttl
.RS
Defines the TTL threshold for the network interface. Packets with a lower TTL than the 
threshols value will be ignored. This setting is optional, and by default the threshold is 1.
.RE

.B altnet
.I networkaddr
\&...
.RS
Defines alternate sources for multicasting and IGMP data. The network address must be on the 
following format 'a.b.c.d/n'. By default the router will accept data from sources on the same
network as configured on an interface. If the multicast source lies on a remote network, one
must define from where traffic should be accepted. 

This is especially useful for the upstream interface, since the source for multicast
traffic is often from a remote location. Any number of altnet parameters can be specified.
.RE

.B whitelist
.I networkaddr
.RS
Defines a whitelist for multicast groups. The network address must be in the following
format 'a.b.c.d/n'. If you want to allow one single group use a network mask of /32,
i.e. 'a.b.c.d/32'. 

By default all multicast groups are allowed on any downstream interface. If at least one
whitelist entry is defined, all igmp membership reports for not explicitly whitelisted
multicast groups will be ignored and therefore not be served by igmpproxy. This is especially
useful, if your provider does only allow a predefined set of multicast groups. These whitelists
are only obeyed by igmpproxy itself, they won't prevent any other igmp client running on the
same machine as igmpproxy from requesting 'unallowed' multicast groups.

You may specify as many whitelist entries as needed. Although you should keep it as simple as
possible, as this list is parsed for every membership report and therefore this increases igmp
response times. Often used or large groups should be defined first, as parsing ends as soon as
a group matches an entry.

You may also specify whitelist entries for the upstream interface. Only igmp membership reports
for explicitly whitelisted multicast groups will be sent out on the upstream interface. This
is useful if you want to use multicast groups only between your downstream interfaces, like SSDP
from a UPnP server.

This option can be combined with
.B blacklist
for fine-grained control.
.RE

.B blacklist
.I networkaddr
.RS
Defines a blacklist for multicast groups. Similar to
.B whitelist
except that if a blacklist entry is defined, all igmp membership reports for
that multicast group will be ignored and therefore not be served by igmpproxy.

Each time a multicast group is forwarded or requested, whitelist and blacklist
entries are evaluated in sequential order, from first to last. The last matching
entry decides what action is taken; if no entry matches the multicast group, the
default action is to serve. Note that, if at least one whitelist entry is
defined before any blacklist entry, all igmp membership reports for not
explicitly whitelisted multicast groups will be ignored.
.RE

.SH EXAMPLE
## Enable quickleave
quickleave
.br
## Define settings for eth0 (upstream)
.br
phyint eth0 upstream 
       altnet 10.0.0.0/8
       
## Disable alternate IP on eth0 (eth0:0)
.br
phyint eth0:0 disabled

## Define settings for eth1 (downstream)
.br
phyint eth1 downstream ratelimit 0 threshold 1

## Define settings for eth2 (also downstream)
.br
phyint eth2 downstream


.SH SEE ALSO
.BR igmpproxy (8)

.SH AUTHOR
Originally written by Johnny Egeland <johnny@rlo.org>
